                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Morphopolis is an artistic little point-and-click indie puzzle game that can be completed in just a couple of hours. Getting 100% of the achievements is very simple because all of them unlock automatically as you progress through the game. The entire experience feels like a small hobby project from the developers. There are absolutely no options or settings in the main menu. You just select a chapter and start playing immediately. There are 5 chapters in total, and each one unlocks right after you finish the previous chapter.\n\nThe artwork is hand-drawn, making the game look very beautiful, colorful, and artistic. There is no real story to follow here. Instead, you just watch and progress through the different transformation stages of a bug. The music and sound design are also well done. A cool trick is that if you want to listen to the tunes from the different stages, you can go into the game files, open the \"sounds\" folder, and directly access the wave files named sfx_music_0 to 5.\n\nHowever, the gameplay has some frustrating issues. Most of the puzzles are on the easier side, but there are a couple of slider puzzles that feel very annoying and frustrating to solve. The movement of the insect can also feel slow and clunky at times. There are no descriptions, tutorials, or voiceovers, so you have to figure out everything by yourself. Another issue is that the game has no save system. If you quit in the middle of a level, you cannot resume from the same point. You are forced to replay that entire chapter from the beginning.\n\nIf you somehow already have this sitting in your Steam library, you can check it out just to enjoy the beautiful art style. Otherwise, you are not missing out on anything special, and it is safe to skip.\n\n[b]My Verdict: 5.5/10[/b]",
